  "status_text": "Nine switch settings can all matter, even when a circuit gets two chances to slip. Ask one light to shine for three settings, go dark for three, then shine for three. Suppose a circuit can change the light once in either direction, with a gate limit that rules out changing twice. Lean checked that all nine settings force more than two misses, and every smaller selection fits (L-lib-1380). In general, one small selection preserves every error-budget decision up to a chosen limit. The search can now use that exact cutoff for one-change light schedules.",
